  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain from thunderstorms could be very heavy over\nthe Laurels and south-central mountains Sunday morning and \nagain later in the day. There is still potential for some heavy \nrain to fall over the Lower Susquehanna Valley and Ridge-and- \nValley region.\n\nVery light showers over eastern OH at 3PM (Sun) are running \nover the slightly drier/more stable air in place over PA. The \nrain shouldn't reach into Central PA until after midnight, and\neven then, little/no risk of heavy rain will exist for us until\naround 4-5 AM. Upstream convection will continue into the Laurel\nHighlands per the preponderance of the newest numerical\nguidance. A 30-35KT westerly LLJet will bring in along and above\na warm front inching north and east in the morning. PWATs get\nabove 2\" as the morning convection dives into MD. The first\nforecast issue is just how far north the heaviest precip will\ntrack. Some recent guidance does indicate a swath of heavy rain\nbeing possible north of the first (southern) tier of counties in\nthe morning. FFG over much of Somerset and Cambria Counties is\nclose to 1\"/hr. While a little more drying is expected before\nthe rain arrives there, the FFG will likely still be close to\nbeing that low when it starts raining. The initial slug of\nthunderstorms will cross the southern tier by 10 or 11 AM. While\nthis first slug may not be enough straw to break the camel's \nback, the piece that may do it and make flash flooding may \narrive in the afternoon. WPC ERO SLGT risk is well into the CWA,\neven up almost to Route 6 across the nrn tier. \n\nAs alluded to...there will _probably_ be a second surge of \ninstability and lift during the late afternoon and evening. The \ncertainty of this second round of storms is dependent on the \nerosion of a stable layer which will likely form after the first\nbatch of rain moves through in the morning. If we do break out \nand get the necessary sun/heating, we should see more tall and \nwet storms. However, the NAM and GFS paint a layer of sfc/llvl \nstability/CIN until very late in the day at KUNV as an example \nand over much of the CWA. The FFG is also a bit higher, and \nground able to handle a little more rain, on the whole, over the\neastern counties. Thus, in collaboration with our neighboring \noffices, we have posted a Flash Flood Watch for Sunday. However,\nwe have started a little smaller than what we view as the \npotentially-eventually threatened area. It was a tough decision,\nwith confidence in both the threat area and timing/redevelopment\nlow. Later shifts will be able to evaluate newer guidance as the\ntime window closes. \n\nThe threat for heavy rain does stop by midnight. So, we have\ndecided to cover both periods of potentially heavy rain with \none watch. While it does stretch across a span of time in mid- \nday and early afternoon when no heavy rain should occur, the\ntime span is pretty short in length.\n\nAdditionally, the potential for strong to severe storms exists\nas long as we have unstable conditions. The hodographs in the\nmorning are actually well-curved in the south/southwest, but\nhave just a meager LLJet. Shear is stronger in the late\naftn/evening. Wind damage is the most probable severe threat,\nbut a spinning storm or two is possible. \n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure yields a short dry window before \nmore storms arrive in mid-week. \n\nA cold front moves through late Sunday night and Monday.\nDewpoints don't drop much at all until later in the day Monday.\nSo, there will still be plenty of time on Monday for instability\nto develop and pop showers and perhaps a thunderstorm or two\nduring the daytime. By sunset, these will be gone as dry air \nand loss of heating kill them off. Dewpoints drop into the 50s\nfor many Tuesday morning. Large scale subsidence should yield a\nmostly sunny day, then. Return flow Tuesday night and Wed will\nbring the humidity up and a cold front will approach on\nWednesday. Storms are expected mainly in the Wed-Wed night\ntimeframe, but speed of the CFROPA will determine if all/part \nof Thursday will be dry. If showers linger, they would be over\nthe south.",
